WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:02.870
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Meg from Seattle.

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:03.870
Meg, welcome.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.270
Speaker 3: Hi, David.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:06.600
Thanks for taking my call.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.689
Speaker 1: My pleasure.

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:14.269
Speaker 4: So you've probably heard the the
phrase that there's no ethical consumption

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:16.570
under capitalism.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.390
I was wondering, do you agree with that, Gary?

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:20.859
Speaker 1: No.

00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:26.369
I mean, listen, they are in general, I don't
agree with almost any of these overarching

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:29.310
absolutes for such broad statements.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.850
There is no ethical consumption under capitalism.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:40.370
I mean, listen, you can you can argue that
that's the case and you can argue that there

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:46.899
there are hardcore free marketeers who say
the only ethical system is free market capitalism.

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:54.250
I find it so unproductive to argue about such
overarching, broad statements, like, I'm more

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:59.630
interested in figuring out how can we improve
the economic system we have so that it works

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:04.860
better for more people and raises up the standard
of living and gets more people health care

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.030
and rewarding well-compensated jobs.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:13.550
I just don't know that we get there by arguing
about whether there's any ethical consumption

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:14.550
under capitalism.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.290
To me, it really seems like a distraction.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.450
Speaker 4: I think that's a good point.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:24.680
And I'm guessing you don't have time to go
into, you know, what alternative, you know

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.750
what alternatives.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.100
You know, you would propose to capitalism.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.100
Like you said, you you're more interested
in looking at what we can do to make things

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.100
better.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.100
Yeah.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.990
Speaker 1: No, I mean, listen, Meg, the the
general outlook I have is that social democracy

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:46.370
in the style of many northern European countries
is the direction I would like to go.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:50.660
I'm okay with markets directing resources
in many areas.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:59.100
I'm okay with there being, you know, a film
and television, live streaming, private industry

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:05.830
and decisions being made by private companies
about what to produce and then selling it

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.550
to Netflix or Hulu or like I'm okay with that.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:09.890
I think that that's okay.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:18.340
I don't need to socialize every industry,
but also we need to be regulating industry

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.130
significantly for the betterment of our planet.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:27.200
We need to be taxing the very rich in order
to fund programs to make sure that no one

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.830
drops below a certain standard of living.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:34.500
We need to be taking certain industries out
of the market, and that includes health care

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:35.500
and others.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:39.670
I mean, that's my general view of how I would
structure things to improve from where we

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:40.670
are now.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:41.670
Speaker 4: Absolutely.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.820
I mean, our economic system is just one piece.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:48.960
Speaker 3: A very big piece of all the systems
that aren't working.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.920
Speaker 4: That are pretty broken.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.530
Speaker 1: Yes, absolutely.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:53.530
Absolutely.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:54.530
Yes.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.950
Like I'm more interested in that than like,
is there ethical consumption under capitalism?

00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:59.360
Call me crazy, Meg.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.420
Speaker 3: Sure, sure.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:02.420
Quick follow up.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:03.420
Speaker 4: Yes.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.860
You know, I've also heard it say that, you
know, that we're in like late stage capitalism.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.510
And I'll be honest, I haven't done a huge
amount of research on that specifically.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.890
But do you do you agree do you think we're
in late stage capitalism?

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:15.860
Speaker 1: I have absolutely no idea.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:19.910
Is that that's you know, there's this whole
movement that makes that claim.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.150
I think we just have to wait and see.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:31.000
And we things are sort of unprecedented in
that modern humanity is a tiny fingernail

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:37.390
on the wingspan of how long humans have been
on this planet and countries.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:44.750
The size and power of today's superpowers
are also basically unprecedented.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.790
And so we are in new territory.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:50.550
And I don't know, it could be that this is
that that we are going to continue improving

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.900
on capitalism for the next thousand years.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.370
It could be that capitalism is 20 years from
collapse.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:56.940
I have absolutely no idea.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:58.170
Speaker 4: Okay.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:03:59.810
Well, thank you so much for taking my call.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:00.810
Speaker 1: David Thank you.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.351
Mag Important questions which I very much
appreciate hearing from you.

00:04:04.351 --> 00:04:05.351
Let's go next to Lucas from Massachusetts.

00:04:05.351 --> 00:04:06.351
Lucas, welcome to the program.

00:04:06.351 --> 00:04:07.351
Lucas from Massachusetts.

00:04:07.351 --> 00:04:08.351
You are muting yourself.

00:04:08.351 --> 00:04:09.351
You have to unmute yourself for me to be able
to hear you.

00:04:09.351 --> 00:04:10.351
I've unmuted you, but you must unmute yourself.

00:04:10.351 --> 00:04:11.351
And last chance for Lucas from Massachusetts.

00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:12.351
You are on, but you must unmute yourself.

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:13.351
In order for me to hear you.

00:04:13.351 --> 00:04:14.351
Right and no Lucas.

00:04:14.351 --> 00:04:14.357
Unfortunately, Lucas wanted to stay muted.

